# benilla.toc — the built-in interface, as an ordinary addon manifest.
#
# The reference's own interface ships as `FrameXML.toc`; ours was a hand-ordered Rust array until
# decision 1178. It is parsed by the same `.toc` parser any third-party addon will be
# (`benilla-ui/src/toc.rs`), and that is the point: a privileged built-in UI is how addon support
# rots. If our own interface loads through the addon path, the addon path works — it is exercised
# by every single run. If it keeps a private door, that path is untested by construction.
#
# The order is LOAD order and it is load-bearing: a template must be defined before the frame that
# inherits it, and a later file paints on top of an earlier one. Every entry's comment is why it
# sits where it sits; those comments are knowledge, not decoration.
#
# The FIRST entry is the font-object registry, and the loader splits there: it alone loads at
# startup, ahead of the glue screens that share our one glyph atlas, and everything after it
# materializes on entering the world (decision 1051).
#
# ── TWO STORES, ONE LIST (decision 1751) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A **bare filename** is a file we ship, from `assets/ui` (compiled into the binary, 1175).
# A **path** — `Interface\FrameXML\ContainerFrame.xml` — is the reference's OWN file, executed off
# the player's installed patch chain. Nothing of Blizzard's is copied here; the player runs the
# file they already own, and it cannot drift from itself.
#
# That is the end state for this interface: the stock 1.12 FrameXML, off the player's chain, and
# `assets/ui` is scaffolding that retires window by window. Migrating one is one line here changing
# from ours to theirs, plus the deletion of our copy — which is why the two stores share ONE
# ordered list instead of the reference getting a private "load everything first" pass. It could
# not have that pass: stock `ContainerFrame.xml` inherits `ItemButtonTemplate`,
# `CooldownFrameTemplate`, `SmallMoneyFrameTemplate` and `UIPanelCloseButton`, so it has to sit
# after the files that declare them, exactly like any other entry.
#
# Order still decides every collision, in both directions. A reference file sourced ABOVE ours has
# its colliding globals overwritten by ours (that is why `PaperDollFrame.lua` is where it is); a
# reference file sourced AT the position of the window it replaces owns its names outright, which
# is what migrating a window means. `ui_script::reference_ui` is the rule; this list is the order.

## Interface: 11200
## Title: benilla

# The named virtual Font objects (decision 0084) — must load first so any FontString's
# `inherits="GameFontNormal"` resolves against a registered object.
# ══ THE FONT REGISTRY — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, 1888) ══
# All 51 `<Font>` objects, name for name, plus the colour codes, `CHAT_FONT_HEIGHTS`,
# `MATERIAL_TEXT_COLOR_TABLE`, `MATERIAL_TITLETEXT_COLOR_TABLE` and `RAID_CLASS_COLORS`.
# Ours was a transcription differing only in formatting (it inlined `<FontHeight>`), plus one
# addition: `ITEM_QUALITY_COLORS`, whose reference home is `UIParent.lua` and which 1888 moved
# to our UIParent.xml so it survives this swap.
Interface\FrameXML\Fonts.xml

# ══ THE STRINGS — the reference's own first line, seated second here (1848) ══
# **Second, not first, and that is deliberate.** Manifest entry 0 is the FONT REGISTRY:
# `load_font_registry` takes entry 0 and `load_ingame_ui` takes the rest, so entry 0 is the boot
# boundary itself (1051). Putting the strings there would move `Fonts.xml` across it.
# `GlobalStrings.lua` is line 1 of the reference's `FrameXML.toc`, and it was missing from ours
# entirely: the app loaded it out of band at Startup (`ui_script::setup_script`, "the VM's first
# load is the real GlobalStrings.lua"), so the running client was right and the MANIFEST was not
# self-sufficient. Every harness that loads the manifest without that pre-step got a VM with no
# strings, which is a gap four separate windows tripped over before anyone looked at the toc.
#
# The stock files read these at LOAD, not just at paint: `Blizzard_MacroUI.xml`'s character tab
# does `format(CHARACTER_SPECIFIC_MACROS, UnitName("player"))` inside its own OnLoad, and a nil
# format string there aborts the handler.
#
# The app's Startup load stays where it is — the cast-fail display (0427) resolves messages before
# the in-game UI materializes at all (1051) — so this is a second, idempotent assignment pass.
Interface\FrameXML\GlobalStrings.lua
# ══ THE LOCALE ADJUSTMENTS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 5) ══
# `Localize()` (which this file also CALLS at load, exactly as the reference's own two-part
# `<Script>` does) and `LocalizeFrames()`. Nothing of ours ever shipped either, and the gap was
# recorded in two places: `GameTime.xml` set `TwentyFourHourTime = 1` itself with a note saying the
# reference sets it in `LocalizeFrames`, and `HelpFrame.xml` names a whole family of strings that
# "`LocalizeFrames()`, which benilla does not ship, so it never fired here either" would have set.
#
# It is the reference's own slot (FrameXML.toc l.3, straight after Fonts.xml) and the order is the
# point: `Localize` overwrites `GlobalStrings.lua` keys, and GlobalStrings is loaded ahead of this
# whole manifest at VM setup (`ui_script/mod.rs`) — which is FrameXML.toc l.1, so the two land in
# the reference's own order. The file it patches is the PLAYER'S, so the locale is theirs by
# construction rather than ours to guess.
#
# `LocalizeFrames()` is called from `UIParent_OnEvent`'s VARIABLES_LOADED arm — the reference's own
# caller (UIParent.lua l.230-235), added to our UIParent.xml with this window.
Interface\FrameXML\Localization.xml
# ══ THE LOCALE LOOKUP — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 19) ══
# `GetText(token, gender, ordinal)` and its three helpers (`GetPluralIndex`/`GetPluralTag`/
# `GetGenderTag`) plus `GenderTagInfo` / `MAX_GENDER_INDICES` / `MAX_PLURAL_INDICES`.
#
# It is a `.lua` entry because the reference has no XML for it: 1.12 pulls it in through
# `<Script file="LocaleProperties.lua"/>` at the top of `UIParent.xml`, and until that window
# migrates this is where it comes from.
#
# WORTH KNOWING, because it saved building the wrong thing: `GetText` LOOKS like an engine binding
# — `reference/1.12-globals.tsv` has it, and the exe's `FrameScript_GetText 0x703bf0` is a real
# function — but the exe's one CALLS this Lua global. So there is nothing to implement; the whole
# selector is fifty lines of FrameXML, and sourcing them is the entire fix. It composes a suffix
# from the gender (`_FEMALE` at 3, and five more tags 1.12 never uses in enGB) and the plural
# (`_P1` for any ordinal that is not 1), then falls back suffix-by-suffix to the bare token.
#
# The reputation page is what needed it — `ReputationFrame.lua:65` reads its standing
# words with `GetText("FACTION_STANDING_LABEL"..id, gender)` — and fourteen more call sites across
# FriendsFrame, StaticPopup and the quest windows are waiting behind it.
Interface\FrameXML\LocaleProperties.lua
# The reference's own second file, in its own position: TEXT/message/_ERRORMESSAGE, the
# ScriptErrors dialog, and the DialogBoxFrame + DialogButton*Texture kit it is built from.
# Straight after Fonts.xml because those are the only things it needs (GameFontRedLarge,
# DialogButtonNormalText/HighlightText) and everything above it in FrameXML.toc is engine.
Interface\FrameXML\BasicControls.xml
# **UIParent itself, and it must come before every frame that declares `parent="UIParent"`** —
# a parent name is resolved at LOAD, and a name that does not exist yet does not error: the loader
# warns and silently falls back to the enclosing frame (`loader/mod.rs`, "names no frame"). With
# this file at its old position below UiPanels.xml, `StaticPopup1`/`StaticPopup2` would have taken
# that fallback and the reparent would have been a silent half-fix.
#
# This is the reference's own slot: FrameXML.toc has BasicControls.xml at l.6 and UIParent.xml at
# l.8, above MoneyFrame.xml (l.10) and StaticPopup.xml (l.23). It needs nothing from the files it
# now precedes — it declares only UIParent and WorldFrame, inherits no template, and its scripts
# (UIPARENT_MANAGED_FRAME_POSITIONS + UIParent_ManageFramePositions, decision 0272, the ref
# mechanism that re-anchors the cast bar / chat over the bottom bars) are called later, applied by
# the loader's post-load bootstrap (ui_script/manifest.rs).
# ══ THE WORLD FRAME — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1983) ══
# WorldFrame.xml + WorldFrame.lua: the <WorldFrame> singleton (the engine's own kind, mouse-enabled
# by construction, its hits the world's), the framerate readout ToggleFramerate/TOGGLEFPS drives
# off GetFramerate, the OnUpdate that ticks StaticPopup and MirrorTimer frames while the UI is
# hidden, and the ScreenshotStatus child ("Screen Captured" over SCREENSHOT_SUCCEEDED/FAILED,
# the 1.5 s GetTime fade, TakeScreenshot for the SCREENSHOT binding). Right above UIParent.xml as
# the reference's toc has it (l.7-8). Needs Fonts.xml's SystemFont above; everything else it
# names is call-time.
Interface\FrameXML\WorldFrame.xml
# UIParent itself and its Lua — the reference's own file (1751; decision 1988), at the slot its
# toc gives it (l.8, right after BasicControls). UIParent.lua is the whole of the glue this
# engine used to carry as UIParent.xml + UiPanels.xml: the UIPanelWindows table and the slot
# manager (Show/HideUIPanel, CloseAllWindows, ToggleGameMenu's ESC ladder), the fade/flash/pulse
# kits (ticked from UIParent's own OnUpdate, so the three driver frames of ours retired with
# it), the managed bottom-stack positions, the model turntable, the LoadOnDemand shims, and the
# sixty-six globally-routed events and their arms. It sources LocaleProperties.lua itself. Its
# OnUpdate also ticks FCF_OnUpdate, UnitPopup_OnUpdate, BattlefieldFrame_OnUpdate, the
# battlefield-position ask (1980) and the ready-check timeout (1989) — call-time names, all of
# them below. LowHealthFrame (parent WorldFrame, above) and OutOfControlFrame ride along.
# What stayed ours moved out: the window tab template and its settle + the placeholder icon
# (ScrollTemplates.xml), 0561's bag verbs (ContainerFrameAdapters.xml), the script log's row
# (ScriptLogFrame.xml), and three aliases in OptionsFrame.xml that answer the reference's three
# options windows and its Cancel button.
Interface\FrameXML\UIParent.xml
# ══ THE ITEM-SLOT BASE — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 24) ══
# `ItemButtonTemplate` and the eight `SetItemButton*` verbs out of `ItemButtonTemplate.lua`. It
# sits as early as it can be read: no frames, no dependencies of its own, and every window that
# paints an item slot inherits or calls it — ours and third-party alike. Their absence is what left
# the director's Bagnon window with a title, a gold line and no bag slots at all.
#
# Our copy called the reference's file "the specification" in its own header and re-implemented the
# eight verbs against it; the specification is now the thing that runs. The template's regions and
# size are identical either way (`$parentIconTexture` / `$parentCount` / `$parentStock` /
# `$parentNormalTexture`, 37x37) — which is what makes this the safest of the foundational swaps
# rather than the widest: everything that inherits it asks for the same four names.
Interface\FrameXML\ItemButtonTemplate.xml
# ══ THE MONEY KIT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1962) ══
# MoneyFrame.lua + MoneyFrame.xml (two rows: the .xml sources no .lua): MoneyTypeInfo, the
# MoneyFrame_* API, MoneyFrameTemplate / SmallMoneyFrameTemplate. Needs only Fonts, and must come
# BEFORE the dialog engine (StaticPopup's coin row inherits SmallMoneyFrameTemplate at LOAD), which
# is the reference's own order too (FrameXML.toc l.9-10 against l.23). The money CURSOR family the
# kit's PickupFunc/DropFunc rows drive (PickupPlayerMoney, DropCursorMoney, GetCursorMoney, the
# trade and send-mail pairs) is this engine's now. 1190's transcription is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\MoneyFrame.lua
Interface\FrameXML\MoneyFrame.xml
# ══ THE MONEY INPUT KIT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 23) ══
# `MoneyInputFrameTemplate` and the ten `MoneyInputFrame_*` verbs out of `MoneyInputFrame.lua` —
# the three-box gold/silver/copper editor a player types an amount into. Nothing of ours ever
# shipped it, and it declares no frames, so this is a pure addition: a template and ten globals.
#
# THE REFERENCE'S OWN SLOT, straight after MoneyFrame.xml (FrameXML.toc l.9-10) and for the same
# reason ours is here — the coin art and `MONEY_ICON_WIDTH` family it lays its boxes out against.
#
# It is groundwork rather than a window, and the windows it is groundwork FOR are named so the
# next session does not have to re-derive it: stock `TradeFrame.xml` and `MailFrame.xml` both build
# their amount entry from this template and call `MoneyInputFrame_SetMode`/`_GetCopper`/
# `_SetCopper`/`_ClearFocus`/`_SetTextColor`/`_SetOnvalueChangedFunc` — six of the names below —
# and neither can be sourced until they exist. (Trade still needs four ENGINE verbs beyond this:
# `AddTradeMoney`, `GetCursorMoney`, `GetTradePlayerItemLink`, `GetTradeTargetItemLink`.)
#
# **TWO entries, and the `.lua` was missing.** This `.xml` declares only the template and does not
# source its `.lua`; the reference's toc lists both (l.11-12). Listing the `.xml` alone brought the
# template and none of the ten verbs — the header above has claimed otherwise since the day it was
# written, and a probe of a full manifest load answered `nil` for every one of them. Same shape as
# `TextStatusBar`, found by sweeping every chain entry for it once that one bit;
# `every_chain_xml_brings_its_own_lua` is now a gate so neither can recur.
Interface\FrameXML\MoneyInputFrame.lua
Interface\FrameXML\MoneyInputFrame.xml
# ══ THE TOOLTIP — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1968) ══
# GameTooltip.xml sources HealthBar.lua and GameTooltip.lua and <Include>s GameTooltipTemplate.xml:
# the two virtual templates (GameTooltipTemplate, ShoppingTooltipTemplate — thirty declared line
# pairs each, the status bar, the money row), the GameTooltip / ShoppingTooltip1 / ShoppingTooltip2
# instances and SmallTextTooltip. The widget itself is this engine's (0274); every verb the file
# calls was already bound. Its slot is the reference's own: FrameXML.toc l.13, after MoneyFrame
# (l.9-10, `SmallMoneyFrameTemplate` resolves at load) and before the panel kit. The order
# requirement the old header named still holds: ItemRef.xml below expands GameTooltipTemplate,
# and so do the 27 corpus addons that name it in an XML inherits=.
#
# What our transcription carried that the reference does not, and where each went (1968): the
# `$parentThicken` plate fill (a look deviation, retired — the plate is the reference's art);
# `SHOW_NEWBIE_TIPS`'s default, which is UIOptionsFrame.lua's and sits in OptionsFrame.xml's uvar
# block with its family; `FACTION_BAR_COLORS`' fallback table — the reference's is
# ReputationFrame.lua's, on the chain below; `NUM_SHOPPING_TOOLTIPS` — PaperDollFrame.lua's.
Interface\FrameXML\GameTooltip.xml
# ══ THE DROPDOWN KIT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══
# UIDropDownMenuTemplate + the DropDownList frames + the whole `UIDropDownMenu_*` API — before its
# first customers (the unit popups below, the world map's pickers, the trainer filter). The stock
# file pulls its own `UIDropDownMenu.lua` and `<Include>`s UIDropDownMenuTemplates.xml, so this one
# line carries all three. We also gain `DropDownList3`, which our transcription stopped short of.
#
# Ours (0203 phase 2, widened for 0434 phase 5) defined every stock global plus six helpers of its
# own. Five were named nowhere outside the file. The sixth, `CloseMenus`, moved to UiPanels.xml —
# the reference keeps it in `UIParent.lua`, not its dropdown file, and UiPanels is both where our
# slice of UIParent.lua lives and ahead of all five of its consumers.
Interface\FrameXML\UIDropDownMenu.xml
# ══ THE UIMenu KIT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 21) ══
# `UIMenuTemplate` and `UIMenuButtonTemplate`, with `UIMenu.lua`'s `UIMenu_OnShow`/`_OnUpdate` and
# the four `UIMenuButton_*` handlers arriving through the file's own `<Script file=>`.
#
# The kit was a block inside our ChatFrame.xml — a transcription that kept the reference's names
# for the addon surface and said so, and whose own note recorded a divergence it had to make (the
# row label authored LEFT-anchored, because the reference leans on 1.12's ButtonText default rect
# and our lazy label slot did not reproduce it).
#
# **Sourcing the file retired only the XML half, and this header claimed both for months.** The ten
# `UIMenu_*`/`UIMenuButton_*` functions stayed in ChatFrame.xml under the reference's own names, and
# that file loads at 939 — so OURS overwrote the chain's from the day this entry landed, the reverse
# of 1855's direction and just as silent. They are gone now; the kit is the reference's end to end
# (1869).
#
# THE POSITION is the reference's own (FrameXML.toc l.12, straight after GameTooltip.xml, which it
# needs for the tooltip backdrop its menu wears). Far above ChatFrame.xml, which is the one thing
# that used it here — resolved at load, and now satisfied earlier rather than in the same file.
Interface\FrameXML\UIMenu.xml
# ══ THE UNIT MENUS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1958) ══
# UnitPopup.xml + UnitPopup.lua: UnitPopupButtons/Menus and UnitPopup_ShowMenu, the nine menus
# and every row's facts — after the dropdown kit it drives (its own position, FrameXML.toc
# l.16), before the unit/party frames whose DropDown children's OnLoad initialize into it. Its
# enable pass runs from UIParent's OnUpdate line, the reference's own hook. 0434's
# transcription is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\UnitPopup.xml
# ══ THE CHAT-LINK TOOLTIP — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 6) ══
# `SetItemRef` (the chat-link click router) and `ItemRefTooltip` / `ItemRefCloseButton`.
#
# THE POSITION IS THE REQUIREMENT: `inherits="GameTooltipTemplate"` is resolved at LOAD, so this
# sits below GameTooltip.xml. The dropdown kit and UnitPopup above it are a call-time need only,
# but the reference's own TOC has the same shape and the order costs nothing.
#
# THREE THINGS OUR COPY CARRIED THAT THE REFERENCE'S DOES NOT, each moved to the reference's own
# home rather than deleted:
# · `FriendsFrame_ShowDropdown` + its initializer, and the `FriendsDropDown` frame they toggle.
#   1.12 keeps all three in `FriendsFrame.lua`/`.xml`; ours had a second private copy here because
#   nothing else called them. FriendsFrame.xml (far below) owns the one body now and publishes the
#   reference's name over it — resolved at call time, so the order does not bind.
# · `ValidateFramePosition`, which the sourced `ItemRef.xml`'s OnDragStop calls (l.48). It lives in
#   `UIParent.lua` in 1.12, so it is transcribed into our UIParent.xml with this window.
# · Two divergences in `SetItemRef` itself, both now the reference's own body: the shift-click arm
#   fills a visible ADD_IGNORE/ADD_FRIEND/ADD_GUILDMEMBER/ADD_RAIDMEMBER popup's editbox before it
#   considers the chat box, and falls through to `SendWho("n-"..name)`. Ours guarded both away as
#   "the friends/who arc, deferred"; `StaticPopup_Visible` and `SendWho` both exist, so the guards
#   were outliving their reason.
#
# AND ONE THING IT GAINS: the reference's tooltip is `movable` and drag-armed, so a chat-link
# tooltip can be dragged off the middle of the screen. Ours declared "benilla has no
# user-moved-frame affordance yet" — `StartMoving`/`StopMovingOrSizing` are bindings
# (`script/object/movable.rs`), so that note was stale too.
Interface\FrameXML\ItemRef.xml
# The shared status-bar numerals machinery (decision 1082): TextStatusBar_* + the opt-in
# template. The ref's own TOC loads TextStatusBar right before its unit frames, same seat.
#
# ══ THE STATUS-BAR NUMERALS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ═══════════
# `TextStatusBar_*` + the `TextStatusBar` template. The reference's own toc loads it right
# before its unit frames; this is the same seat.
#
# Nothing of ours inherits the template — it exists for the reference's windows, whose rows
# carry `inherits="TextStatusBar"` and would have found ours. What blocked the swap was a
# signature WE widened (`TextStatusBar_Initialize`), fixed by giving our own bars the OnLoad
# the reference's have.
#
# **TWO entries, because the reference has two** (its toc l.32-33). This `.xml` declares only the
# template and does NOT source its `.lua` — unlike most stock windows, whose `<Script file=>`
# brings their code in. Listing the `.xml` alone loads a template whose OnLoad calls a function
# nothing defined: every `TextStatusBar_*` name reads nil, every guarded call is a silent no-op,
# and the numerals simply never appear. The `.lua` comes first, as it does there.
Interface\FrameXML\TextStatusBar.lua
Interface\FrameXML\TextStatusBar.xml
# ══ THE PLAYER AURA BAR — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══
# The HUD row of buff/debuff icons under the minimap, driven by the UnitAura feed
# (`crate::ui_aura`; decisions 0255/0257).
#
# FIRST OF THE KIT, which is the reference's own order (its toc: BuffFrame 40, CombatFeedback 41,
# UnitFrame 43, PlayerFrame 44, PartyFrame 45, TargetFrame 46, PetFrame 47). It has to precede
# PartyFrame because stock `PartyMemberFrame.lua:60` calls `RefreshBuffs` from each row's OnLoad
# and `BuffFrame.lua` is where that lives. Ours had BuffFrame below the unit frames; that
# inversion is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\BuffFrame.xml
# ══ THE UNIT-FRAME KIT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 22) ══
# `UnitFrame.xml` declares NO frames — it is a `<Script file="UnitFrame.lua"/>` and nothing else —
# so it is a pure addition of thirteen globals: `UnitFrame_Initialize`, `_Update`, `_OnEvent`,
# `_OnEnter`, `_OnLeave`, `_OnUpdate`, `_UpdateManaType`, the `UnitFrameHealthBar_*` /
# `UnitFrameManaBar_*` pairs, and `GetUnitName`. `UnitFrame_Initialize` is what stock
# `PlayerFrame.xml`, `TargetFrame.xml`, `PartyFrame.xml` and `PetFrame.xml` each call in their
# OnLoad.
#
# Until the four windows below arrived, our own UnitFrames.xml loaded underneath this and
# redefined four of the thirteen — `_Update`, `_OnEvent`, `_OnEnter`, `_OnLeave` — so the
# reference's bodies were dead the whole time and stock party rows would have called OUR update,
# which indexed a `nameText` the reference's rows do not carry. That file is gone; these are live.
Interface\FrameXML\UnitFrame.xml
# The same shape and the same reason. `CombatFeedback.xml` declares no frames either: three timing
# constants plus `CombatFeedback_Initialize` / `_OnCombatEvent` / `_OnUpdate`, the floating
# hit/miss text a unit frame's portrait carries. Two of the three used to collide with bodies our
# UnitFrames.xml owned; with that file gone the reference's three are what run.
Interface\FrameXML\CombatFeedback.xml
# ── The four unit windows themselves (1751, window 22) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# `PlayerFrame` + `PlayerStatusGlow` + `PlayerPlayTime` + `PlayerPVPIconHitArea` +
# `PlayerFrameGroupIndicator` + the dropdown, out of `PlayerFrame.lua`.
Interface\FrameXML\PlayerFrame.xml
# `PartyMemberFrame1..4`, `PartyMemberBuffTooltip` and `PartyMemberBackground`, out of
