# value is the PLAYER's locale rather than our assumption about it.
Interface\FrameXML\GameTime.xml
# ══ THE ZONE SPLASH — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 8) ══
# Two files, in the reference's own order and for its own reason.
#
# `FadingFrame.lua`'s kit first (`FadingFrame_OnLoad`/`_Show`/`_OnUpdate` and the three
# `_Set*Time` setters). Our copy inlined it into ZoneText.xml on the argument that
# ZoneTextFrame/SubZoneTextFrame are its only consumers in the whole 1.12 FrameXML — true, and
# still the wrong shape once the file could simply be sourced: an addon that wants a fading frame
# reaches for the kit's published names, not for the splash. The reference gives it its own TOC
# line (l.25) and so do we. It declares no frames at all — it is a `<Script file=>` and nothing
# else — so it needs nothing above it.
Interface\FrameXML\FadingFrame.xml
# …then the splash itself: `ZoneTextFrame` / `SubZoneTextFrame` / `AutoFollowStatus`, `SetZoneText`
# and the three `Zone*Duration` constants, driven by the ZONE_CHANGED family `crate::area` fires
# and by AUTOFOLLOW_BEGIN/END. Needs Fonts (ZoneTextFont/SubZoneTextFont) + UIParent, both far
# above; kept by the minimap cluster, the other consumer of the zone-text host globals.
#
# Our copy's own header called its Lua "OURS: our own implementation of the behaviour
# ref-ZoneText.xml specifies", with the published names and the measurements quoted. The
# reference's file is that specification, so it replaces it whole — including the
# `CONTESTED_TERRITORY`/`FACTION_CONTROLLED_TERRITORY`/`FREE_FOR_ALL_TERRITORY`/`AUTOFOLLOW*`
# strings, which came from ours as `X = X or "…"` fallbacks and now come from the player's own
# GlobalStrings.lua like every other string in this interface.
Interface\FrameXML\ZoneText.xml
