# no `.lua`, no `<Include>` — and a descendant of UIPanelTemplates (`OptionsCheckButtonTemplate`
# inherits `UICheckButtonTemplate`, `OptionsButtonTemplate` inherits `UIPanelButtonTemplate`), so
# it stays seated after that file.
#
# `framexml-transcription-diff.py` reports no handler our transcription was missing, and the swap
# GIVES BACK the reference's own inline hover: ours had factored it into a shared
# `BenillaOptionsWidget_ShowTooltip`, where the reference open-codes it once per widget.
Interface\FrameXML\OptionsFrameTemplates.xml
# …and ours, shrunk to the ONE template the chain's file does not carry:
# `UIOptionsCheckButtonTemplate`, whose reference home is `UIOptionsFrame.xml` — a file we do not
# build, because benilla's options window is `OptionsFrame.xml`, a different design. The corpus
# names it at 8 `CreateFrame` sites, so it cannot simply go. Same basename as the entry above by
# design: bare = ours, path = the reference's. Retires when UIOptionsFrame.xml migrates.
OptionsFrameTemplates.xml
# ══ THE COLOUR DIALOG — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 11) ══
# `ColorPickerFrame` (a `<ColorSelect>`), `OpacitySliderFrame`, `ColorPickerOkayButton`,
# `ColorPickerCancelButton`, and the separate `OpacityFrame` beside them. The file ships no `.lua`
# — its whole script is inline, and the one function that drives it
# (`UIDropDownMenuButton_OpenColorPicker`) lives in UIDropDownMenu.lua.
#
# Last of the "shared FrameXML surface with no benilla consumer yet" cluster that runs from
# UIPanelTemplates.xml down, and the swap is exactly why that posture was worth holding: **86 of
# the 218 corpus addons reach this window** (64 of those files are `Dewdrop-2.0.lua`, which drives
# THIS one rather than rolling its own), and nothing we ship opens it — so every one of them is now
# talking to the reference's own frames rather than to our re-expression of them.
#
# THE SEAT IS A REQUIREMENT, not tidiness: the Okay/Cancel buttons
# `inherits="GameMenuButtonTemplate"`, resolved at LOAD, and that template lives in
# UIPanelTemplates.xml far above (it moved there from GameMenuFrame.xml — the reference's own
# file for it — precisely because GameMenuFrame.xml is deliberately LAST, which is also why this
# entry does not follow the reference's own position below it). Everything else it needs is far
# above: Fonts' GameFontNormal trio, and UiPanels' ShowUIPanel/HideUIPanel plus its UISpecialFrames
# entry and the ESC rung that cancel-clicks it. UIDropDownMenu.xml sits above too, but that
# direction is call-time only, so the two could sit either way round.
Interface\FrameXML\ColorPickerFrame.xml
