# sprite; its WorldMapPing is a MinimapPing.mdx Model pane this engine holds and draws nothing
# into (named gap, 1980).
Interface\FrameXML\WorldMapFrame.xml
# ══ THE CAST BAR — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══
# `CastingBarFrame` + its Border/Flash/Spark/Text children, driven by the SPELLCAST_* events
# `crate::ui_cast` fires (decision 0137 phase 1). Our `CastingBar.xml` was a transcription of this
# very file under a different name — all five frame names identical — so the swap is a deletion.
#
# The file SOURCES ITS OWN LUA (`<Script file="CastingBarFrame.lua"/>` at its l.3), which is why
# only the `.xml` is listed here and `every_chain_xml_brings_its_own_lua` is satisfied.
#
# It brings `CastingBarFrame_UpdatePosition`, which ours never had. Only needs Fonts before it.
Interface\FrameXML\CastingBarFrame.xml
# ══ THE MIRROR TIMERS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 7) ══
# `MirrorTimer1..3`, `MIRRORTIMER_NUMTIMERS`, `MirrorTimerColors` and the `MirrorTimer_Show` /
# `MirrorTimerFrame_*` family out of `MirrorTimer.lua`. The breath/fatigue bars at top-centre,
# driven by the `MIRROR_TIMER_*` events `crate::ui_mirror` fires off the wire. Needs only Fonts
# (GameFontHighlight) before it; kept beside the cast bar, whose border art it shares.
#
# ALL THREE of our copy's recorded divergences resolved at the swap (1751 §2), and two of them had
# already stopped being true:
# · THE START DRIVER. Ours hosted `MIRROR_TIMER_START` on a hidden `MirrorTimerDriver` frame
#   because "our UIParent.xml is script-only — it has no frame of its own to register events on".
#   It has one now, so the registration and the `MirrorTimer_Show` call moved into
#   `UIParent_OnEvent` where the reference keeps them (UIParent.lua l.97 + l.374-377), and the
#   extra frame is gone.
# · THE POOL WALK. The reference asks its two questions in two loops and bounds the SECOND by
#   `STATICPOPUP_NUMDIALOGS` — a copy-paste from StaticPopup.lua, and its own bug. Ours folded
#   them into one walk to avoid it. Now faithful, which forced the real fix in the right place:
#   our `STATICPOPUP_NUMDIALOGS` was 2 against the reference's 4, so the third mirror bar could
#   never be claimed by a new timer. UiPanels.xml grew instances 3 and 4 with this window.
# · THE PAUSE ARG is now the reference's own bug, reproduced: `MirrorTimerFrame_OnEvent` reads
#   `arg1` as a timer NAME and then as a number (`arg1 > 0`), so a real MIRROR_TIMER_PAUSE raises.
#   1.12 never reaches it — vmangos refuses to send that packet and substitutes a full START,
#   saying so in `Player::SendMirrorTimers` — and `ui_mirror.rs`'s Pause edge carries the note.
Interface\FrameXML\MirrorTimer.xml
# ══ THE DURABILITY ALERT — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 4) ══
# The "armor guy" under the minimap: `DurabilityFrame`, its eleven glyph textures, and
# `DurabilityFrame_SetAlerts` / `_OnUpdate` with `INVENTORY_ALERT_STATUS_SLOTS` and
# `INVENTORY_ALERT_COLORS`, all from `DurabilityFrame.lua` through its own `<Script file=>`.
#
# Ours was a measurement-faithful transcription and it is deleted. The swap re-examined its one
# recorded divergence (1751 §2) and made it faithful: our copy OMITTED the ref's `OnUpdate`,
# because the whole body is an `enchantTimer` countdown and that timer is provably dead in 1.12
# (`SetAlerts` nils it; the only line that sets it is commented out, and so are the two colours it
# would have painted). The reference runs the handler anyway, so we do too — the cost is one call
# per frame on one frame, and it is the reference's own behaviour rather than our judgment of it.
#
# THE POSITION: `parent="UIParent"` and `relativeTo="MinimapCluster"` are both resolved at LOAD, so
# this sits below UIParent.xml and MinimapCluster.xml. It is far above the reference's own slot
# (FrameXML.toc l.86) and that is fine — nothing else reaches it at load, and its seat is decided
# at runtime regardless: like every managed HUD frame the authored anchor is dead on arrival, with
# `UIParent_ManageFramePositions` re-seating it on the loader's post-load pass and on every
# OnShow/OnHide (ref UIParent.lua l.1758-1768).
#
# `UpdateInventoryAlertStatus` is the one global the sourced file names that this client does not
# bind. It is reachable only from the dead `enchantTimer` branch above, so it is left absent rather
# than stubbed (1203) — the honest state is "nothing calls it", not "we answer it with a lie".
Interface\FrameXML\DurabilityFrame.xml
# ══ THE ERROR TOASTS AND THE RAID WARNING — the reference's own (1751, window 14) ══
# `UIErrorsFrame` (the red/yellow toast strip) and `UIErrorsFrame_OnLoad`/`_OnEvent`, then
# `RaidWarningFrame` and `RaidBossEmoteFrame` with `RaidWarningFrame_OnLoad`/`_OnEvent`. Two files,
# in the reference's own split and its own order: the warning frame anchors `relativeTo=
# "UIErrorsFrame"`, resolved at LOAD, so it cannot precede it.
#
# Our single `ErrorsFrame.xml` held both and is deleted. It declared `RaidWarningFrame` with **no
# `<Scripts>` at all**, and the reason it gave was wrong: *"we already deliver that chat type
# through `ui_chat::feed`, so wiring a second consumer would print every raid warning TWICE"*.
# Checked rather than inherited — `ui_chat` fires `CHAT_MSG_RAID_WARNING` as an ordinary chat event
# and nothing of ours consumes it for a display. So the reference's handler adds the big centre
# text and its `PlaySound("RaidWarning")` **beside** the chat line, which is exactly what 1.12
# does: the chat window and the warning frame are two consumers of one event there too.
#
# It also brings `RaidBossEmoteFrame`, which our copy withheld on the "no corpus caller, and a
# shadowed name with no caller is building against a guess" rule (1222). That rule is about names
# WE would invent; this one is the reference's own declaration, so there is nothing left to guess.
#
# `ChatTypeInfo["RAID_WARNING"]` comes from the reference's ChatFrame.xml far below — read at call time, so the
# order does not bind.
Interface\FrameXML\UIErrorsFrame.xml
Interface\FrameXML\RaidWarning.xml
# ══ THE BAG WINDOWS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (decision 1751, window 1) ══
# `ContainerFrame1..12`, `ContainerFrameTemplate`, `ContainerFrameItemButtonTemplate` and the whole
# `ToggleBag`/`ToggleBackpack`/`OpenAllBags`/`IsBagOpen`/`ToggleKeyRing` verb family — the real
# 1.12 file, running as the file, with `ContainerFrame.lua` arriving through its own
# `<Script file=>`. Our `BagFrame.xml` used to build five windows here; it is the bag BAR only now,
# and the bank's six popouts (BankFrame.xml) became ContainerFrames at ids 5..10, which is what the
# reference does.
#
# THE POSITION IS THE REQUIREMENT, and it is why 1751 moved sourcing into this manifest at all:
# this file's `inherits=` are resolved at LOAD and reach four templates above it —
# `ItemButtonTemplate`, `SmallMoneyFrameTemplate` (MoneyFrame.xml), `CooldownFrameTemplate`
# (Cooldown.xml), `UIPanelCloseButton` (UIPanelTemplates.xml) — plus `parent="UIParent"`. It
# cannot load "first" the way a Lua-only sourcing had to.
Interface\FrameXML\ContainerFrame.xml
# ══ THE CHARACTER WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (decision 1751) ══
# `CharacterFrame` + its five-tab row and portrait/name header (CharacterFrame.xml), the paper doll
# itself — model pane, 19 equipment slots, the ammo slot, the five attributes, the melee and ranged
# stat blocks, the five resistances (PaperDollFrame.xml) — and the pet page over the same templates
# (PetPaperDollFrame.xml). Our `CharacterFrame.xml` (2027 lines) and `PetPaperDollFrame.xml` (773)
# stood here and are deleted.
#
# THREE FILES IN THE REFERENCE'S OWN ORDER, and every edge between them is resolved at LOAD:
#   - CharacterFrame.xml declares `CharacterFrame`, which the other two name in `parent=` (1734).
#   - PaperDollFrame.xml declares `StatFrameTemplate`, `AttributeFrameTemplate` and
#     `MagicResistanceFrameTemplate`, which PetPaperDollFrame.xml inherits on 11 frames.
#   - PaperDollFrame.xml also declares `PaperDollItemSlotButtonTemplate`, which stock
#     `MainMenuBarBagButtons.xml` below inherits on all five bag buttons.
#
# THAT LAST EDGE IS WHY THE BLOCK SITS HERE rather than below the bag bar where ours did.
# `ItemSlotButtonTemplates.xml` held a transcribed copy of that template for exactly one reason:
# our CharacterFrame.xml loaded after the bag bar and could not declare it in time. The reference's
# own toc has no such problem (CharacterFrame 53, PaperDollFrame 54, MainMenuBarBagButtons 59), so
# the copy is deleted and the reference's declaration is the live one — the same retirement
# `BagSlotButtonTemplate` took one window earlier.
#
# `PaperDollFrame.lua` used to be a manifest line of its own, sourced far above everything so that
# our bodies would win its 18 collisions. It arrives through PaperDollFrame.xml's own
# `<Script file=>` now, at this position, and the collisions went with the file that had them.
#
# What the block needs from above, all far up: Fonts.xml (every `inherits=` font object),
# ItemButtonTemplate.xml (the slot template's base, and the `SetItemButton*` family),
# Cooldown.xml (`CooldownFrameTemplate` + `CooldownFrame_SetTimer`), GameTooltip.xml, UiPanels.xml
# (`CharacterFrameTabButtonTemplate`, `UIPanelWindows`), UIPanelTemplates.xml (`UIPanelCloseButton`,
# `UIPanelButtonTemplate`, and `PanelTemplates_SetNumTabs`, which `CharacterFrame_OnLoad` calls at
# LOAD), TextStatusBar.xml (`SetTextStatusBarTextPrefix`, called at load too, plus the
# `TextStatusBar` the pet page's XP bar inherits), UnitFrames.xml (`PlayerFrameHealthBar` /
# `PlayerFrameManaBar`, named in that same OnLoad), MainMenuBar.xml (`MainMenuExpBar`, likewise),
# MainMenuBarMicroButtons.xml (`MicroButtonTooltipText`, the five tab hovers) and UIParent.xml
# (`Model_OnLoad`/`_RotateLeft`/`_RotateRight`/`_OnUpdate` — the model pane's turntable, the
# reference's own code living in our UIParent.lua counterpart).
#
# Two names it writes live BELOW it, and that is fine because both are runtime rather than load:
# `HonorLevelText` and `HonorGuildText` (HonorFrame.xml), which `PaperDollFrame_SetLevel` and
# `_SetGuild` fill "while we're at it" — reached only from OnShow and the UNIT_* events, long after
# that file has loaded.
Interface\FrameXML\CharacterFrame.xml
Interface\FrameXML\PaperDollFrame.xml
# Tab 2, the reference's file as well. `PetTab_Update` shows or hides `CharacterFrameTab2` off
# `HasPetUI()` and re-anchors tab 3 over the gap, so a classless character's row has no hole.
Interface\FrameXML\PetPaperDollFrame.xml
# ══ THE BAG BAR — the reference's own, off the player's chain (decision 1751, window 3) ══
# `MainMenuBarBackpackButton`, `CharacterBag0Slot`..`CharacterBag3Slot`, `KeyRingButton`,
# `BagSlotButtonTemplate` and the `BagSlotButton_*`/`BackpackButton_*`/`Disable_BagButtons` family
# — the real 1.12 file, with `MainMenuBarBagButtons.lua` arriving through its own `<Script file=>`.
# It also owns `KEYRING_CONTAINER = -2`, which is where the reference puts it (l.1).
#
# Our `BagFrame.xml` stood here and is deleted. Its own header named the condition it was waiting
# on — "replaced by the reference's own file the day `PutItemInBag` / `PickupBagFromSlot` are
# bound" — and both are bindings now (`script/cursor/bag_verbs.rs`), so the ref's own click arms
# work: its `PutItemInBag(id)` is the equip/swap our `BenillaBagBarSlot_Place` open-coded.
#
# THE POSITION IS THE REQUIREMENT: `inherits="PaperDollItemSlotButtonTemplate"` on the four bag
# slots and `inherits="ItemButtonTemplate"` on the backpack are resolved at LOAD, and so is
# `parent="MainMenuBarArtFrame"` — so this sits below the character-window block (above, which is
# where stock `PaperDollFrame.xml` declares that template) and below MainMenuBar.xml (which declares
# the art frame). It sat below a transcribed copy of the template in `ItemSlotButtonTemplates.xml`
# until the character window migrated and made that copy redundant (decision 1810). Being BELOW the sourced ContainerFrame.xml is
# legibility, not necessity, in either direction: every verb each file calls of the other resolves
# at call time, `KEYRING_CONTAINER` included (no top-level read of it exists in ContainerFrame.lua).
#
# TWO THINGS THE SWAP DID NOT INHERIT, and both live in ContainerFrameAdapters.xml below rather
# than being edited into anything of Blizzard's: the item-push card (0887 — the reference's is a
# `<Model>` playing an .mdx and this engine renders no FrameXML models, so ours samples that
# model's own tracks and draws a quad), and the GlobalStrings fallbacks the install-less harnesses
# need. The keyring's SHOW trigger moved the other way — into the main bar's file, which is exactly
# where the reference keeps it (MainMenuBar.xml l.323-341; stock since 1938).
Interface\FrameXML\MainMenuBarBagButtons.xml
# The stack-split spinner (decision 0216 §6/slice 2): loaded beside the bag windows, whose slot
# click handler (the reference's `ContainerFrameItemButton_OnClick`) calls `OpenStackSplitFrame`.
# Order between the two doesn't matter for correctness (Lua globals resolve at call time, and
# neither file's XML `inherits=` reaches into the other's templates) — adjacent for legibility.
Interface\FrameXML\StackSplitFrame.xml
# Our ADAPTERS over the reference's own container files — one wrapper, for the keyring tooltip
# detour benilla answers differently (0765). Must load AFTER them, since it captures at load the
# body it wraps; its own header says what it stopped needing to do when the XML became sourceable.
ContainerFrameAdapters.xml
# The Reputation tab: the same separate-top-level-file shape as the two pages around it, seated
# BETWEEN them because that is the real client's tab order (Character 1, Pet 2, Reputation 3,
# Skills 4 — ref CharacterFrame.xml:79-168), and the page's `id=` is that slot. Its hard
# dependencies are all far above: CharacterFrame.xml (the anchor target it is pinned to by name,
# and the tab row it raises tab 3 of), ScrollTemplates.xml (the faux list + its trough template),
# UIPanelTemplates.xml (`UIPanelCloseButton`, the detail popup's X — this page is that shared
# template's first benilla consumer), OptionsFrameTemplates.xml (`OptionsCheckButtonTemplate`, two
# of the three detail boxes), GameTooltip.xml (the column newbie tips) and Fonts.xml. One
# dependency runs the other way and is worth naming: the WATCH BAR half of the reference's
# ReputationFrame is declared in this same stock file (since 1875's co-edit, below) — so `FACTION_BAR_COLORS` and
# `ReputationWatchBar_Update` are already defined when this page paints a row or ticks the
# "Show as Experience Bar" box.
# ══ THE REPUTATION PANE — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, scoped by 1844) ══
# One line replaces one of ours: 47 of our 48 declared names were already the reference's, and the
# one that was not (`ReputationListScrollFrameScrollBarTrough`) is our own scroll kit's. Nothing
# gated it — the stock file inherits its own two row templates plus `UIPanelCloseButton`, fonts and
# `OptionsCheckButtonTemplate`, and that last came off the chain with 1841.
#
# THE CO-EDIT was compulsory. The reference declares the reputation WATCH BAR in this same file;
# ours lived in `ActionBar.xml` behind a note defending the placement. Frame globals publish
# NON-overwritingly and ActionBar.xml was manifest line 340 against this file's — so ours would have
# kept the name and the chain's bar would have been built and unreachable, which is exactly what
# `GroupLootFrame` did for months (1838). The bar, its update, its two text verbs,
# `MAX_PLAYER_LEVEL` and `FACTION_BAR_COLORS` all went home with it (1875).
Interface\FrameXML\ReputationFrame.xml
# ══ THE SKILLS TAB — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1956) ══
# SkillFrame.xml + SkillFrame.lua: CharacterFrame's fourth page (`parent="CharacterFrame"`,
# `id="4"`), the twelve-row list over 0437's skills seam (GetNumSkillLines/GetSkillLineInfo's
# twelve-value tuple, the expand/collapse pair, the selection), the detail bar, the unlearn
# button (its UNLEARN_SKILL dialog lives with the other dialogs in UiPanels.xml). At the
# reference's own slot, after ReputationFrame and before HonorFrame. 0437's transcription is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\SkillFrame.xml
# The Honor tab: the same separate-top-level-file shape as the three pages above it, and LAST of
# them because it is the last of the tabs (the real client's 5, ref HonorFrame.xml:5) — which
# CLOSES the row: the character window's five tabs are now the reference's five, in its order, at
# its numbers, with nothing left past the end. Its hard dependencies are all far above:
# CharacterFrame.xml (the anchor target it is pinned to by name, the tab row it raises tab 5 of,
# and the `CharacterNameText` its level line hangs under), GameTooltip.xml
# (`BenillaTooltip_AddNewbieTipFor`, which all five of its hovers go through) and Fonts.xml
# (GameFontGreenSmall/GameFontRedSmall carry the honorable/dishonorable colouring, and
# HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR the tip titles).
# It must come BEFORE InspectFrame.xml, and that order is a REQUIREMENT rather than tidiness: the
# inspect window's own honor page inherits the five `HonorFrame*ButtonTemplate` row templates
# declared here, and `inherits=` resolves at LOAD. The reference has the identical edge — its
# `HonorFrameTemplates.xml` is `<Include>`d by `HonorFrame.xml` and inherited by the LoadOnDemand
# `Blizzard_InspectUI`, which loads later still.
Interface\FrameXML\HonorFrame.xml
# ══ THE LoadOnDemand ADDONS — Inspect, Talent, Macro, Raid: NO ROW HERE (1751; 1967) ══
# `Blizzard_InspectUI`, `Blizzard_TalentUI`, `Blizzard_MacroUI` and `Blizzard_RaidUI` are not
# listed, exactly as the reference's own `FrameXML.toc` lists no addon: each is `LoadOnDemand: 1`
# on the player's chain — inside patch.MPQ; the loose `Interface\AddOns\Blizzard_*\` folders on
# disk hold one `.pub` decoy each, which is what kept these four windows on transcriptions for
# months — and `ui_script::addons` seats every such addon as a registry row (1957) that
# `UIParentLoadAddOn` loads the first time its opener runs. The openers are the reference's own,
# in UIParent.xml, its home for them: `InspectUnit` → `InspectFrame_LoadUI`, `ToggleTalentFrame`
# → `TalentFrame_LoadUI`, `ShowMacroFrame` → `MacroFrame_LoadUI`, and the stock RaidFrame.lua's
# `RaidFrame_LoadUI` on the first roster update. 1832, 1833, 1848 and 1874 took the four off the
# chain but listed them here with everything else, and left the LoadUI calls out rather than
# stub them (1203); 1967 gives them the reference's load timing and the calls back.
#
# What those headers recorded that still holds: the inspect honor page inherits HonorFrame.xml's
# five row templates, the macro icon chooser inherits `ClassTrainerListScrollFrameTemplate`
# (1862) — FrameXML files listed above, so resolved long before any addon loads, which is also
# why the reference's own toc has the same order. The gates over chain files
# (`ui_script::reference_ui`) walk these addons' files after the manifest's entries — a reached
# addon is part of the shipped interface, row or no row.
# ══ THE DRESSING ROOM — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1969) ══
# DressUpFrame.xml + DressUpFrame.lua: the ctrl-click item preview (1060). Its `<DressUpModel>`
# is a real widget kind now — PlayerModel's three verbs plus its own `TryOn`/`Dress`/`Undress`
# (table 0x84f190), queued as intents the app composes the booth's look from — and its pane draws
# the "dressup" booth by the reference's own name (`portrait::model_pane_booth`). The rotate
# buttons and OnUpdate drive `Model_RotateLeft/Right/OnUpdate` from UIParent.xml above, and the
# Close/Reset buttons inherit UIPanelCloseButton/UIPanelButtonTemplate from the panel kit above.
# Slot as before: after the windows whose slots feed it; `DressUpItemLink` resolves at call time.
Interface\FrameXML\DressUpFrame.xml
# ══ THE SPELLBOOK — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1952) ══
# SpellBookFrame.xml + SpellBookFrame.lua: the book, the twelve page buttons, the skill-line tabs,
# the Spell/Pet toggle tabs. Its whole engine surface was already ours (1924's UpdateSpells, 1931's
# GetSpellTabInfo tuple); what stopped it in 1926 — MultiActionBar_ShowAllGrids/HideAllGrids on
# show/hide — has been the reference's MultiActionBars.lua since 1938. Loaded here, after the
# action-bar files it calls into and after MainMenuBarMicroButtons (UpdateMicroButtons on show). The 'P' binding
# calls its own ToggleSpellBook(BOOKTYPE_SPELL). 0216's transcription is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\SpellBookFrame.xml
# The one adapter the reference's file needs from this engine: its pet-page autocast shine is a
# <Model> (UI-AutoCastButton.mdx) and this engine's <Model> draws no pixels; the shine benilla
# draws is its own token texture (1383/1392/1393). BENILLA_ALLOW_OWN_UI=1.
SpellBookAdapters.xml
# ══ THE SOCIAL WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1959) ══
# FriendsFrame.xml + FriendsFrame.lua: the O-key window's four tabs — friends/ignore, who, the
# guild roster with its control and info panes, and the raid tab whose pane is the stock
# RaidFrame.xml right below (the reference's own file split, its toc l.71-72). Over the social
# API (`benilla-ui/src/script/social.rs`) and `crate::ui_social`'s feed/drain, the guild seam,
# the party roster. Its twelve dialogs live with the other dialogs in UiPanels.xml; the
# GUILD_INVITE arms are UIParent.xml's, the reference's own home. 0668/1257/1549's transcription
# is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\FriendsFrame.xml
# ══ THE RAID PANE — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, scoped by 1839) ══
# The social window's fourth tab, in its own file because RaidFrame is a child of FriendsFrame and
# a child cannot be declared before its parent — the reference splits it the same way and for the
# same reason (its FrameXML.toc has RaidFrame.xml on the very next line). Decision 1549.
#
# The pane is the entry here; the LoadOnDemand half that carries the group frames and the
# tear-off pullouts, `Blizzard_RaidUI`, is a registry row that the stock RaidFrame.lua loads on
# the first roster update (the addons header far above; 1967). 1839 measured the swap — 83 of our
# 84 frame names were
# already the reference's, 20 of our 22 Lua functions were, and NOTHING needed building. The one
# name that was not is `BenillaRaidDropDown`, and the reference has no raid drop-down at all: it
# reuses `FriendsDropDown`, which FriendsFrame.xml declares four manifest lines above.
#
# GAINED: `RaidPulloutFrameTemplate`, `RaidPulloutButtonTemplate`, `RaidBuffButtonTemplate` and the
# fifteen `RaidPullout*` verbs — the tear-off group pullouts we never transcribed.
Interface\FrameXML\RaidFrame.xml
# ══ THE GOSSIP WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══════════════════
# No engine verb, no widget method, no shadowed name — all three instruments clean. It was
# deferred once on `GossipResize` reading `GetTextHeight()` in the same repaint that sets the
# label, our measurement being a frame late; that is what the swap tests.
Interface\FrameXML\GossipFrame.xml
# ══ THE VENDOR WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══════════════════
# It took three things, in this order: `PickupMerchantItem` (the vendor cursor, 1797), the
# `BenillaMoney_*` coin kit moving out to MoneyFrame.xml where eight other windows can reach it
# without loading this one, and `SetMerchantCompareItem` (1802) — which the gap report could not
# see at all until 1798 taught it to read `:Method(` sites, and which would have raised on the
# first vendor-row hover with every check green.
#
# That is why the notes below say "grouping" rather than "reuses that file's helpers": the
# windows still cluster here, but nothing depends on this file any more.
Interface\FrameXML\MerchantFrame.xml
# ══ THE MAIL WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1970) ══
# MailFrame.xml + MailFrame.lua: the inbox, the open letter (with the auction invoice pane and
# the letter-copy button), the send tab with its stationery picker and recipient autocomplete,
# over the Era mail API (`benilla-ui/src/script/mail.rs`) + `crate::ui_mail`'s feed/drain. What
# the swap built: the stationery family (`GetNumStationeries`/`GetStationeryInfo`/
# `SelectStationery`/`GetSelectedStationeryTexture`, wow-re `stationery-bindings.md`) and the two
# events the stock file listens for that nothing fired — `MAIL_SEND_INFO_UPDATE` on an attachment
# change and `CLOSE_INBOX_ITEM(index)` when a take empties a mail. Its templates are all on the
# chain above: the panel kit, ItemButtonTemplate, MoneyFrame/MoneyInputFrame, UIRadioButtonTemplate
# (UIPanelTemplates.xml), InvoiceTextFontNormal (Fonts.xml) and FriendsFrameTabTemplate
# (FriendsFrame.xml, which the reference's own toc also seats before this, l.71 → l.86).
Interface\FrameXML\MailFrame.xml
# ══ THE TRADE WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1966) ══
# TradeFrame.xml + TradeFrame.lua: both columns' seven slots over the trade API
# (`benilla-ui/src/script/trade.rs`) + `crate::ui_trade`'s feed/drain, the recipient's
# SmallMoneyFrame (TARGET_TRADE) and the player's MoneyInputFrame, the accept glows. Its slot
# tooltips are this engine's SetTradePlayerItem / SetTradeTargetItem; its per-slot events
# (TRADE_PLAYER_ITEM_CHANGED / TRADE_TARGET_ITEM_CHANGED) the feed's. Loaded here, after
# ItemButtonTemplate, MoneyInputFrame, the money kit and the dialog engine it inherits from.
# 0592's transcription is gone.
Interface\FrameXML\TradeFrame.xml
# ══ THE ITEM-TEXT READER — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 9) ══
# `ItemTextFrame` and its page chrome, over the `ItemTextGet*` seam
# (`benilla-ui/src/script/item_text.rs`) and `crate::ui_item_text`'s feed/drain. Letters (mail-made
# permanent copies) and, later, books and plaques. Right after MailFrame, the same arc; needs
# Fonts/UiPanels/ScrollTemplates, all above.
#
# ONE THING OUR COPY CARRIED THAT THE REFERENCE'S DOES NOT, moved to the reference's own home:
# `GetMaterialTextColors` and the two colour tables it reads. 1.12 keeps the function in
# `UIParent.lua` and the tables in `Fonts.xml`, and both of ours now do the same — under the
# reference's published names (`MATERIAL_TEXT_COLOR_TABLE` / `MATERIAL_TITLETEXT_COLOR_TABLE`)
# rather than the `BENILLA_`-prefixed pair this file kept privately. Fonts.xml's own note said
# those two tables would arrive "the day something does" read them; the sourced file is that day.
Interface\FrameXML\ItemTextFrame.xml
# ══ THE TRAINER WINDOW — the reference's own LoadOnDemand addon, off the chain (1751; decision 1957) ══
# No row: `Blizzard_TrainerUI` is the first Blizzard addon benilla loads the way the reference
# does — on demand, from UIParent.xml's TRAINER_SHOW arm through `ClassTrainerFrame_LoadUI` →
# `UIParentLoadAddOn` → `LoadAddOn`, off a registry row `ui_script::addons` reads from the
# chain (its toc, its `## SavedVariables`, its ADDON_LOADED at the end of ITS load). The timing
# is the mechanism: the list packet resets the engine's filter mask, and the addon's
# ADDON_LOADED arm re-pushes the saved filters after it — which eager loading cannot do. Its
# list inherits `ClassTrainerListScrollFrameTemplate` from
# `Interface\FrameXML\ClassTrainerFrameTemplates.xml`, far below, resolved at the addon's
# load. 0237/0247/0251's transcription is gone.
# ══ THE BANK WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (decision 1751, window 2) ══
# `BankFrame`, `BankFrameItem1..24`, `BankFrameBag1..6`, `BankFramePurchaseInfo` and the
# `BANK_CONTAINER`/`NUM_BAG_SLOTS`/`NUM_BANKGENERIC_SLOTS`/`NUM_BANKBAGSLOTS` constants — the real
# 1.12 file, running as the file, with `BankFrame.lua` arriving through its own `<Script file=>`.
# Our own 890-line BankFrame.xml is gone with it, and so are its six popout windows: a bank bag is
# an ordinary `ContainerFrame` at container id 5..10, which is what the reference always did.
#
# THE POSITION IS THE REQUIREMENT, as it was for ContainerFrame.xml. This file's `inherits=` reach
# four templates above it — `ItemButtonTemplate`, `UIPanelCloseButton` (UIPanelTemplates.xml),
# `UIPanelButtonTemplate` (same), `SmallMoneyFrameTemplate` (MoneyFrame.xml) — plus
# `parent="UIParent"`, and its `OnHide` calls `updateContainerFrameAnchors` out of
# ContainerFrame.lua. All are loaded above.
#
# What it needed from this client, all built for it: `PutItemInBag`/`PickupBagFromSlot` (the two
# verbs its bag buttons ARE), the bank-bag inventory band at live ids 64..=69 that gives those
# verbs an item to move, `GetInventorySlotInfo("Bag1".."Bag6")` for the empty-slot art, and
# `StaticPopupDialogs["CONFIRM_BUY_BANK_SLOT"]` re-homed into UiPanels.xml (the reference keeps it
# in StaticPopup.lua, which this client replaces).
#
# One reference quirk carried rather than fixed: `BankFramePurchaseButton` is declared
# `virtual="true"` INSIDE `<Frames>`. Only a top-level element is ever a template — nested nodes
# are materialized regardless (`framexml.rs`) — so the button exists and works, which is what the
# real client does too. Classic Era's Vanilla BankFrame.xml still carries the same attribute on
# the same button, and that button works there.
#
# Kept in the "NPC session windows grouped" cluster beside Trainer/Taxi, and after MerchantFrame.
Interface\FrameXML\BankFrame.xml
# ══ THE FLIGHT MAP — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ══
# The flight-master session (decision 0484 phase 2), driven by the taxi engine seam
# (`benilla-ui/src/script/taxi.rs`) over `crate::ui_taxi`'s feed/drain — unchanged; only the window
# in front of it is the reference's now. Our 557-line transcription carried the same five stock
# globals plus eight helpers of its own, none of which anything outside that file named.
#
# The one thing it needed was the `<TaxiRouteFrame>` widget type, which is registered now (1828) —
# a `CSimpleFrame` and nothing else, so it is `FrameKind::Frame` and the route lines stay what the
# reference makes them: plain `<Texture>` children rotated by an 8-argument `SetTexCoord`.
#
# Pulls only `TaxiFrame.lua`, and still needs only Fonts/UiPanels/GameTooltip (SetTooltipMoney),
# all loaded above — so it keeps its seat in the "NPC session windows grouped" cluster.
Interface\FrameXML\TaxiFrame.xml
# The stable-master window (decision 1676): the hunter's three-slot pet stable over the stable
# API (`benilla-ui/src/script/stable.rs`) + the app's feed/drain. Kept in the "NPC session
# windows grouped" cluster beside Trainer/Bank/Taxi. Two of its dependencies are resolved at
# LOAD and so must sit above it: MoneyFrame (its purse and cost displays inherit
# SmallMoneyFrameTemplate) and UIPanelTemplates (UIPanelButtonTemplate / UIPanelCloseButton).
# A third is resolved at load too: the model pane is a `<PlayerModel>` whose own <OnLoad> calls
# the reference's `Model_OnLoad` (UIParent.xml, far above). That used to be CharacterFrame.xml's
# `BenillaPaperDollModel_OnLoad`, which is why this entry sat below the character window; it no
# longer has to, and stays here only because the NPC-session cluster is where it belongs. The rest
# (ItemButtonTemplate's SetItemButtonTexture, GameTooltip, UiPanels' ShowUIPanel/HideUIPanel,
# UIParent's BuildListString) are resolved when the window repaints, so their order is free.
#
# ══ THE REFERENCE'S OWN, off the player's chain (1751) ══
# A true 1:1 swap: our transcription and the stock file declare the SAME frame names and the SAME
# four globals (`PetStable_OnLoad`/`_OnEvent`/`_Update`/`_NoPetsAllowed`), so nothing that names
# either moves — `ui_stable.rs`, `names.rs` and `net/apply/npc.rs` all reach it by stock names
# already, and `portrait`'s booth keys on `PetStableModel`, which both spell the same.
Interface\FrameXML\PetStable.xml
# ══ THE AUCTION HOUSE — `Blizzard_AuctionUI`, a LoadOnDemand addon: NO ROW HERE (1751; 1971) ══
# The three-tab doublewide window is the reference's own addon off the player's chain, seated as a
# registry row (1957) and loaded on the first AUCTION_HOUSE_SHOW through UIParent.xml's own
# `AuctionFrame_LoadUI` arm (ref UIParent.lua l.154-156, l.575-587), exactly like the trainer,
# inspect, talent, macro and raid addons above. Its engine surface was already whole (decision
# 1511's auction API); what it needed loaded is on the chain above: the panel kit, MoneyFrame /
# MoneyInputFrame, UIDropDownMenu, and the dressing-room pane it embeds — `<DressUpModel
# name="AuctionDressUpModel">`, the 1969 widget kind, drawing the same booth as the dressing room.
# ══ THE CRAFTING WINDOWS — `Blizzard_TradeSkillUI` and `Blizzard_CraftUI`, LoadOnDemand: NO ROW HERE (1751; 1973) ══
# The trade skill window (Blacksmithing, Tailoring, … and Poisons) and the craft window (Enchanting,
# Beast Training) are the reference's own addons off the player's chain, registry rows (1957) loaded
# on the first TRADE_SKILL_SHOW / CRAFT_SHOW through UIParent.xml's `TradeSkillFrame_LoadUI` /
# `CraftFrame_LoadUI` arms (ref UIParent.lua l.166-168, l.190-192, l.605-632). Both inherit the
# ClassTrainerFrameTemplates.xml kit far above and register their own panel rows. What 1973 built:
# the four item-link verbs (`GetTradeSkillItemLink`, `GetTradeSkillReagentItemLink`,
# `GetCraftItemLink` — the `|Henchant:` link — and `GetCraftReagentItemLink`, wow-re
# `tradeskill-craft-item-links.md`) and `SetHyperlink`'s enchant leg. 0437's transcriptions are gone.
# ══ THE LOOT WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751) ════════════════════
# It never needed an engine VERB. It needed a widget TYPE: `<LootButton>` is a real registered
# `CreateFrame` type (decision 1799), and registering it as a plain Button would have loaded the
# file and left every row dead — 1.12 has no Lua verb for "take slot N".
#
# Its ONE method, `SetSlot`, is also what the gap report's new `method=` column found from the
# call site while wow-re was deriving it from the vtable (1798). Both columns read 0 now, and
# every verb the stock file calls at runtime — not just at load — was checked by hand against
# the bindings before this line changed.
Interface\FrameXML\LootFrame.xml
# ══ THE QUESTGIVER WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, 1944) ══
# Stock QuestFrame.xml sources QuestFrame.lua and INCLUDES QuestFrameTemplates.xml (the panel,
# item and title-button templates the quest log below inherits too), so one row brings the
# three-file unit 1913 measured. Its `QuestFrameItems_Update` paints all three windows' reward
# grids — the shared function that made the quest log un-migratable alone until the money went
# stock (1937). Needs, all above: Fonts (QuestFont/QuestTitleFont), MoneyFrame (the two
# MoneyFrameTemplate frames, resolved at LOAD), UiPanels, UIPanelTemplates. Its two saved-variable
# flags (QUEST_FADING_DISABLE, AUTO_QUEST_WATCH) are declared where the reference declares them —
# the options window's init (OptionsFrame.xml, far above).
Interface\FrameXML\QuestFrame.xml
# The two guild-charter windows (decision 1672): the guild registrar — the window the "How do I
# form a guild?" gossip row opens — and the charter itself, the sheet nine people sign. **Both are
# the reference's own files off the player's chain now** (1751, windows 12 and 13). Loaded here,
# right after QuestFrame, because both wear the QuestGreeting parchment and its QuestFont /
# QuestTitleFont objects and read as that family; they need UiPanels (the panel manager seats both,
# and the RENAME_GUILD dialog the charter raises is registered there, where 1.12 keeps every
# dialog), MoneyFrame (the registrar's price rides MoneyFrameTemplate, resolved at LOAD — decision
# 1580's ordering rule) and UIPanelTemplates (the close/action buttons), all far above. The
# registrar must come first: no load-order dependency links them, but the flow does, and the pair
# reads in the order it is used.
Interface\FrameXML\GuildRegistrarFrame.xml
# ══ THE GUILD CHARTER — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 12) ══
# `PetitionFrame` and its nine signature rows, `PetitionFrame_Update`, `MAX_PETITION_SIGNATURES`,
# and the leader/signer button pair that share one anchor. Ours was the same window under the same
# names (decision 1672) with a different expression of the same painter; the reference's file is
# what that expression was written from.
#
# The seat is unchanged and every dependency it names is above: UiPanels (the panel manager seats
# it, and its RENAME_GUILD dialog rides the shared popup engine), MoneyFrame, UIPanelTemplates (the
# close/action buttons `inherits=` at LOAD), and the QuestGreeting parchment family it shares with
# GuildRegistrarFrame directly above.
Interface\FrameXML\PetitionFrame.xml
# ══ THE QUEST LOG — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, 1944) ══
# Stock QuestLogFrame.xml sources QuestLogFrame.lua; the 'L' binding calls its ToggleQuestLog(),
# and its reward hovers are GameTooltip:SetQuestLogItem / SetQuestLogRewardSpell (engine, 1944).
# After QuestFrame.xml (QuestItemTemplate, QuestMoneyFrame_OnLoad, QuestFrameItems_Update),
# MainMenuBarMicroButtons.xml (QuestLog_OnShow calls UpdateMicroButtons unguarded) and the same kit as the
# giver; the reference's toc has the pair at l.53-54.
Interface\FrameXML\QuestLogFrame.xml
# The timed-quest countdown window under the minimap (decision 1150): the reference's own
# file split. After MinimapCluster (its anchor), UIParent (the manage pass that re-seats it,
# and SecondsToTime), GameTooltip (its row hover), and QuestLogFrame — whose window a row
# click opens, and whose MAX_QUESTS it shares.
Interface\FrameXML\QuestTimerFrame.xml
# ══ THE CHAT WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1948) ══
# ChatFrame.xml (ChatFrameEditBox, ChatMenu/EmoteMenu/LanguageMenu/VoiceMacroMenu, ChatFrameMenuButton,
# the frame/tab/edit-box templates, ChatFrame.lua) then FloatingChatFrame.xml (ChatFrame1..7, their tabs
# and dock regions, the tab dropdown, FloatingChatFrame.lua) — the reference's pair in its own order
# (FrameXML.toc l.48-49). What they call and we built for them: the window record verbs and the colour
# registry (benilla-ui script/chat_window.rs, chat_types.rs), the channel verbs (channel.rs), the
# language walk (chat_send.rs), the slash-handler verbs (chat_misc.rs), and UpdateColorByID. Our own
# transcription (0288's ChatFrame.xml) is gone; benilla's instrument commands (/chattest, /shot, …)
# register through SlashCmdList from ScriptLogFrame.xml's dev block, and the app's chat feed FIRES the
# CHAT_MSG_* events the reference's ChatFrame_OnEvent prints from — it no longer prints them itself.
Interface\FrameXML\ChatFrame.xml
Interface\FrameXML\FloatingChatFrame.xml

# ══ THE WORLD-STATE FRAMES — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1972) ══
# WorldStateFrame.xml + WorldStateFrame.lua: the always-up readout 1586 built (the tower/flag/base
# counters at top-centre, `UPDATE_WORLD_STATES` from `crate::world_state_ui`), the capture bars,
# and the battleground SCOREBOARD ours never had — `WorldStateScoreFrame`, over the score family
# 1972 built (`MSG_PVP_LOG_DATA`, the eleven verbs, `UPDATE_BATTLEFIELD_SCORE`) and UIParent.xml's
# `BattlefieldMinimap_LoadUI`. Needs Fonts, GameTooltip, the panel kit (its close button, tabs,
# faux scroll frame) — all above. It sat beside the mirror timers until 1988: the always-up
# frame's OnLoad runs the stock UIParent_ManageFramePositions, and that pass indexes half the
# HUD unguarded — ReputationWatchBar, QuestTimerFrame, QuestWatchFrame (QuestLogFrame.xml),
# MinimapCluster, DurabilityFrame, the bars and their sliding textures — and ends by calling
# FCF_DockUpdate and re-anchoring the container frames. So this file sits after all of them,
# which is why the reference's own toc has it at l.89, near the end (1988).
Interface\FrameXML\WorldStateFrame.xml
# ══ THE MACRO WINDOW — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 32) ══
# A LoadOnDemand addon with no row here since 1967 (the addons header far above); what stays in
# this slot is the FrameXML file it needs loaded first.
#
# Nothing to build. 1835 recorded `SaveMacros` as a missing engine verb and it is not one — the
# only occurrence in the reference's own file is COMMENTED OUT (`--SaveMacros();` in
# `MacroFrame_OnHide`); the triage that found it was not stripping Lua comments.
#
# It was BLOCKED until 1846: `MacroPopupScrollFrame` inherits FrameXML's
# `ClassTrainerListScrollFrameTemplate`, which inherits `FauxScrollFrameTemplate` — and ours was a
# same-named `<Frame>` with a Benilla slider that shadowed the reference's `<ScrollFrame>`. With the
# panel kit on the chain that shadow is gone, so this window went from blocked to a plain swap.
#
# `ShowMacroFrame` moved to UIParent.xml, the reference's own home for it — see 1848.
#
# **`ClassTrainerFrameTemplates.xml` is not optional, and this header used to name the dependency
# without listing the file.** `MacroPopupScrollFrame` — the icon chooser's list — inherits its
# `ClassTrainerListScrollFrameTemplate`, and a missing template is a loader WARNING, not an error
# (1833): the frame is still built, just BARE, with no scroll bar and no `<OnLoad>`. So
# `ScrollFrame_OnLoad` never ran, `this.offset` was never seeded, and the reference's
# `FauxScrollFrame_GetOffset` (`return frame.offset`, no `or 0` — our deleted copy had that
# fallback and was hiding this) handed `MacroPopupFrame_Update` a nil to multiply. Clicking
# "Change Name/Icon" raised. The reference's own `FrameXML.toc` loads this at its line 92, well
# before any addon; ours takes it here — its one consumer, `Blizzard_MacroUI`, is a LoadOnDemand
# row now (1967) and loads after everything. Decision 1862.
Interface\FrameXML\ClassTrainerFrameTemplates.xml
# The script error log (decision 1495): the window `/errors` opens — the list B293's two
# reporters asked for, which the reference's one-message-at-a-time ScriptErrors modal cannot
# be. Ours entirely (1.12 has no counterpart), reading the retained log on the model rather
# than any per-frame feed. Needs ScrollTemplates (BOTH kits — the faux one for the fixed row
# list, the real one for the traceback pane), UIPanelTemplates (the Clear/Close buttons) and
# UiPanels (ShowUIPanel/HideUIPanel + the UIPanelWindows row that seats it and gets it closed
# by ESC). Before GameMenuFrame, which is deliberately last.
ScriptLogFrame.xml
# The GM help window + the ticket-status toast (the HelpFrame.xml transcription): the third
# **center**-area UIPanelWindows row, so it opens like the game menu and the options window and
# shuts every other panel on the way in. It heads the SHELL TAIL for that reason — Help, then the
# options pair, then the game menu last — and it goes ahead of KeyBindingsPage.xml rather than
# between it and OptionsFrame.xml, because those two are a hard must-load-before pair and nothing
# should be wedged into it. Every dependency is far above: UiPanels (ShowUIPanel/HideUIPanel, the
# UIPanelWindows row, and the StaticPopup engine its three ticket dialogs register into),
# UIPanelTemplates (UIPanelCloseButton, UIPanelButtonTemplate2, GameMenuButtonTemplate and the
# real UIPanelScrollFrameTemplate the ticket editor is built on), ScrollTemplates (the faux kit
# behind the GM category list, its shared trough, and the ScrollingEdit pair), GameTooltip (the
# toast wears the tooltip backdrop colours at OnLoad) and MainMenuBarMicroButtons (whose Help button calls this
# file's ToggleHelpFrame, and whose UpdateMicroButtons this file calls back on show/hide — both
# resolved at call time, so that pair could sit either way round). The one seat that is a REAL
# requirement rather than legibility is **after BuffFrame.xml**: the toast re-anchors
# TemporaryEnchantFrame out of its own way, and that frame is declared there.
#
# **The reference's own since 1889.** Ours was 1017 lines against the reference's 2088 — the
# largest shortfall of any window we shipped — so this swap is mostly ARRIVAL, not replacement.
# The file sources its own `HelpFrame.lua` (its l.3), so only the `.xml` is listed. Its one
# named need, `GMSurveyFrame_LoadUI`, is now in UIParent.xml where the reference declares it.
Interface\FrameXML\HelpFrame.xml
# The cinematic frame: the ESC that skips a fly-by, and the SCREENSHOT chord it hands back. It
# draws NOTHING — the letterbox moved to Bevy UI nodes in `crate::cinematic` (decision 1699),
# because the lane this frame paints into is dark for the whole shot.
# Needs UiPanels.xml far above for ShowUIPanel/HideUIPanel and its own UIPanelWindows row; needs
# nothing else, and nothing needs it — the engine reaches it purely by firing CINEMATIC_START.
# Seated late because it is an outermost, screen-owning layer, like the two windows below it.
Interface\FrameXML\CinematicFrame.xml
# ══ THE TUTORIAL FRAME — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1976) ══
# TutorialFrame.xml + TutorialFrame.lua: the alert buttons `TUTORIAL_TRIGGER` queues, the window
# a click opens over `TUTORIAL_TITLE<n>`/`TUTORIAL<n>`, the "Display Tips" checkbox whose unticked
# hide calls ClearTutorials, and the CINEMATIC_STOP arm that clicks the Welcome alert when the
# intro ends. Right after CinematicFrame.xml as the reference's toc has it (l.76-80); its
# engine — the two flag banks, the fire-once trigger, the four verbs — is `crate::tutorial`.
# Needs UIPanelTemplates (UICheckButtonTemplate), BasicControls (the dialog button textures),
# GameTooltip, and UiPanels' SetButtonPulse, all far above.
# ══ THE TABARD DESIGNER — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751; decision 1977) ══
# TabardFrame.xml + TabardFrame.lua: the <TabardModel> pane (its ten methods are the engine's,
# `script/tabard.rs`), the five customization rows over CycleVariation, the emblem cells the two
# texture setters paint, Save/CanSaveTabardNow and CloseTabardCreation; opened only by the wire
# (`crate::ui_tabard`). Right before TutorialFrame.xml as the reference's toc has it (l.79-80).
# Needs UIPanelTemplates (UIPanelButtonTemplate, UIPanelCloseButton), MoneyFrame
# (SmallMoneyFrameTemplate), GameTooltip and UiPanels, all far above.
Interface\FrameXML\TabardFrame.xml
Interface\FrameXML\TutorialFrame.xml
# The game menu (decision 0674): the frame ESC opens. LAST on purpose — it is the outermost
# layer of the shell, it depends on everything it touches being loaded (UiPanels' popup engine
# + panel manager, MainMenuBarMicroButtons' UpdateMicroButtons, BagFrame's Disable_BagButtons), and nothing
# depends on it: the ESC chain and the micro button both reach it by name at call time.
#
# NOT yet on the chain, and the reason is not in the gap report: the stock file's buttons reach
# `UIOptionsFrame`, and stock `UIParent.lua:997` indexes it — so the ESC menu drags in the whole
# options family, the same chain `MainMenuBarMicroButtons` is deferred behind. All three columns
# read clean because the file LOADS clean; what it needs is a sibling window, not a verb.
#
# Its CAMP/QUIT dialogs and their event driver HAVE moved out, to UiPanels.xml — the reference's
# own homes (StaticPopup.lua l.564/586 for the dialogs, UIParent.lua l.304-315 for the three
# events). The ESC menu never owned them; it was just where ours were first needed. That is one
# less thing to move when the options family lands.
GameMenuFrame.xml
